      "onOwnerNotCompiled": "BODY_LANGUAGE_OWNER_NOT_COMPILED",
      "onOwnerUnenumerated": "BODY_LANGUAGE_OWNER_UNENUMERATED",
      "onOwnerAmbiguous": "BODY_LANGUAGE_OWNER_AMBIGUOUS",
      "onOwnershipMissing": "BODY_LANGUAGE_OWNERSHIP_REQUIRED",
      "selectionLaw": "The dialect is the EFFECTIVE edition of the SELECTED compilation target that owns this body path: the unit targetEdition when it states one, otherwise the universe edition map entry for its crateName. There is NO fast path - Cargo lets a target override its package default, so package defaults that all agree still do not determine a body dialect - and a clones fact over any Rust universe therefore requires the committed SourceUnitOwnershipV1 whatever the package map looks like. The order of decision is fixed and each step has its own cause: (1) no committed ownership refuses; (2) enumeration partial refuses BEFORE any row is looked at, so incomplete discovery can never act as an implicit edition selection by hiding a conflicting owner; (3) rows are those whose path EQUALS the enclosing fact anchor path - never a prefix, never a nearest directory, never a first match - and no row at all refuses as not-compiled; (4) rows are then restricted to selectedUnitIds, and a path compiled only by unselected targets refuses as not-selected rather than borrowing an out-of-scope edition; (5) SELECTED owners whose effective editions AGREE are admissible, which is the ordinary shared-file and lib-plus-test-target case; (6) selected owners that DISAGREE are an ambiguous unselected request and refuse. Because selectedUnitIds is committed inside the record whose H identity the universe names, each selection is a DIFFERENT sourceUniverse: one physical path compiled at two editions is validly analysed under each, one selection at a time. Selection establishes the JOIN only - no path, crate name, unit id or selection enters the body-language-version record.",
      "alwaysRequired": true,
      "onOwnerNotSelected": "BODY_LANGUAGE_OWNER_NOT_SELECTED",
      "scopeVersusEnumeration": "SELECTED SCOPE and INCOMPLETE ENUMERATION are different claims and neither substitutes for the other. A selection says these targets ARE the analysis, so an owner outside them is deliberately out of scope and its edition does not bear on this body; the selection is committed in the identity, so it is visible to every consumer and changes the universe. Incomplete enumeration says the producer did not finish, so an owner INSIDE the selected scope may exist that is not listed and could contradict a listed one; that is a gap in knowledge, it is not visible as a selection, and it therefore admits no body dialect at all. Silently omitting a conflicting owner from the units table is not a selection either: an omission leaves no trace, whereas a selection is part of the universe identity. Partial evidence stays useful under the existing law rather than voiding a Run: the clones scope simply mints no body identity, its Coverage carries the incompleteness, the predicate is indeterminate and the seal is indeterminate."
     },
