    "note": "The universe of the syntax-only mode. `semantic universe` names the thing that DETERMINES a fact, and for a grammar-only analysis that is the selected bundled-grammar set, not a compiler semantic model. It exists because the capability matrix advertises declares/literal/control-flow at `syntactic` and clones at `normalized-body-hash` for syntax-only, and because file/package/vcs-change inventory facts must be representable in a repository that contains no TypeScript and no Rust unit at all: fact2 and subject-scope both REQUIRE a universe h-identity from this closed domain set, so with only the two compiler universes registered those advertised cells could mint no fact and no scope. Making the universe fields nullable was rejected: it would delete the invariant that every fact states what produced it, and would leave `which grammar read this body` unanswerable for exactly the facts that depend on it. Semantic capability is NOT widened by this domain - a syntax-only universe can carry only the syntactic and inventory rungs and the clones rung; imports/references/calls/types/reachability remain language-tier-unsupported, and a universal negative still requires its own complete Coverage.",
    "languageVersionBinding": {
     "record": "#/$defs/body-language-version",
     "encoding": "raw-32-byte-sha256-of-canonical-record",
     "retention": "derived",
     "fields": {
      "compilerName": {
       "source": "native-context",
       "path": [
        "grammarBundle",
        "parserName"
       ]
      },
      "compilerVersion": {
       "source": "native-context",
       "path": [
        "grammarBundle",
        "parserVersion"
       ]
      },
      "compilerBuild": {
       "source": "native-context",
       "path": [
        "grammarBundle",
        "bundleDigest"
       ]
      }
     },
     "componentLaw": "body-language-version names THE COMPONENT THAT INTERPRETS THE BODY SPAN - explicitly not the build tool and not the resolver. Under syntax-only that component is the bundled grammar's parser, so the three fields are bound to the retained, closure-joined grammar bundle rather than to a toolchain. No static constant and no fabricated compiler identity is used: parserVersion is joined by admission to the grammar closure's manifest semanticVersion exactly as the compiler versions are, and bundleDigest is the exact retained bundle identity. A body read by the grammar therefore has a DIFFERENT languageVersion from the same bytes read by tsc or rustc, so their clone identities differ. That is required, not incidental: equating a grammar parse with a compiler parse would assert an interpretation that was never performed.",
     "dialect": {
      "key": "grammarVariant",
      "source": "anchor-path-suffix",
      "form": "closed-suffix-table",
      "table": {
       ".rs": "rs",
       ".d.ts": "ts-declaration",
       ".ts": "ts",
       ".tsx": "tsx",
       ".mts": "mts",
       ".cts": "cts",
       ".js": "js",
       ".jsx": "jsx",
       ".mjs": "mjs",
       ".cjs": "cjs"
      },
      "onUnknown": "BODY_LANGUAGE_GRAMMAR_VARIANT_UNKNOWN",
      "selectionLaw": "A syntax-only analysis has no compilation unit, so it has no edition to select and no program to join; its only per-body axis is the variant the bundled grammar selects from the body's own suffix. Longest matching suffix wins, so .d.ts is never read as .ts. A suffix with no bundled grammar REFUSES rather than being folded into a neighbouring variant - which is the same conservative rule the compiler universes use, and it is what keeps `unsupported-file / no-bundled-grammar` an honest classification rather than a silent reinterpretation. Crucially there is NO edition branch here: a .rs body read without Cargo has no admitted ownership, and inventing one would be exactly the fabricated compiler semantics this mode exists to avoid."
     },
     "excluded": {
      "languageMode": "configuration and unit assembly, not the language",
      "anchorPath": "the path establishes the variant and then does not enter the record",
      "normalizerSpecificationDigest": "the clone normalisation level specification is already a separate framed component; it is not the interpreter of the span",
      "grammarSelection": "which grammars this analysis selected is committed in the universe identity; it is not a property of one body"
     },
     "bodyLanguages": [
      "typescript",
      "javascript",
      "rust"
     ],
     "bodyLanguageByVariant": {
      "rs": "rust",
      "ts": "typescript",
      "tsx": "typescript",
      "ts-declaration": "typescript",
      "mts": "typescript",
      "cts": "typescript",
      "js": "javascript",
      "jsx": "javascript",
      "mjs": "javascript",
      "cjs": "javascript"
     },
     "bodyLanguageLaw": "The body language is the language of the BODY, from the same suffix selector, and is closed to the body-language-version languageId enum. The bundled grammar set and that enum are held equal by an explicit drift check, so a newly bundled grammar cannot start producing bodies of an unregistered language and the advertised `any file whose extension maps to a bundled grammar` cannot silently outrun what the identity record can express."
    }
   }
  },
